## Step 8: Decide whether to resolve OSCI bot's results thread

OSCI bot's own `<!--RESULTS_COMMENT-->` note
is itself a resolvable discussion thread (unlike every other OSCI bot note on the
MR). When the gating pipeline fails, this thread is left unresolved and blocks
merging until a human (or this agent's decision, applied outside it) resolves it.

You only decide the boolean `resolve_osci_discussion` here -- finding the actual
discussion thread and resolving it is Python's job afterwards (it independently
looks up the right thread for this pipeline run; it never trusts an ID from you),
so do not try to call `get_merge_request_discussions` yourself to find or resolve that thread (the history lookup in Step 5 is the only use you have for it).

- **Set `resolve_osci_discussion: true`** only if every failed/errored gating test
  job's assessment from Step 5 is exactly `Infrastructure issue` or `Test bug` (a
  broken/flaky test, not a regression in the change under review). This is the one
  case where this agent proposes resolving a thread without a human reviewing first
  -- deliberately narrower than Step 5's own philosophy (its own threads always
  require a human), because this thread isn't one this agent opened: it's OSCI
  bot's, and OSCI bot itself auto-resolves it the moment a later pipeline run is
  clean, so resolving it here for the same underlying reason (the failure has
  nothing to do with the change under review) is consistent with how the thread
  already behaves.
- **Leave `resolve_osci_discussion: false`** (the default) if any failure's
  assessment is `Real failure` or `Flaky test` that couldn't be confidently
  attributed to infrastructure, if no confident assessment was reached, or if
  nothing failed at all (OSCI bot resolves its own thread on a clean run, so there's
  nothing for this agent to do). A human must resolve a genuinely open thread
  themselves after reviewing Step 5's posted analysis.
- If different jobs failed for different reasons, treat it as one all-or-nothing
  decision -- set it `false` if *any* failure isn't clearly infra/test-bug.
- Always state in your `summary` whether you're proposing to resolve the OSCI
  results thread and why (or leaving it unresolved and why), so this decision
  doesn't have to be re-derived later.
